.TH XOUT286 4 "August 2026" "Substrate" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
xout286 \- Microsoft x.out 80286 segmented executable format (SCO Xenix/286)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B x.out
is the executable format of Microsoft Xenix and SCO Xenix.  One magic number
covers three unrelated targets \(em 8086, 80286 and 80386 \(em so the
processor field, not the magic, decides which loader claims a file.  This
page describes the 80286 flavour, loaded by
.I sys/exec/formats/xout286.c
and executed under the
.B PERS_SCO_X286
personality (see
.BR sco_x286 (4)).
.PP
An 80286 x.out image is a
.I 16-bit protected mode
program.  Xenix gave each of its segments a private LDT descriptor and the
linker baked the resulting selectors into the binary, so the segments are not
relocatable: a middle-model program reaches its own code with
.B lcall $0x47,$off
and expects selector 0x47 to be the segment the linker assigned.
.SH HEADER
The file begins with a 32-byte
.B struct xexec
followed by a
.BR "struct xext" ,
both shared with the 386 format (see
.IR sys/exec/formats/xout.h ).
The fields that select this loader are:
.TP
.B x_magic
0x0206 for every x.out variant.
.TP
.B x_cpu
Processor: the low nibble is 0x04 for the 8086, 0x09 for the 80286 and 0x0a
for the 80386.  Bit 0x20 marks a middle or large model image \(em one with
more than one code segment.
.TP
.B x_renv
Run-time environment flags.  This loader requires
.B XE_SEG
(0x0800, a segment table is present) and
.B XE_EXEC
(0x0001, fully linked).
.TP
.B x_entry
Entry offset within the segment named by
.BR xe_eseg .
.TP
.B xe_segpos ", " xe_segsize
File offset and byte size of the segment table.  Entries are
.B struct xseg
at a 32-byte stride.
.SH SEGMENTS
Each
.B struct xseg
gives a type
.RB ( XS_TEXT " or " XS_DATA ),
the selector the linker assigned
.RB ( xs_seg ,
with TI=1 and RPL=3, so 0x3f is LDT slot 7), the file offset and physical
size of its image, and its virtual size.  No segment may exceed 64 KiB,
because a 16-bit descriptor cannot describe more.
.PP
Substrate maps every segment into its own naturally-aligned 64 KiB linear
window and fills the LDT slot the binary expects with a 16-bit (D/B clear),
byte-granular descriptor based there.  Segment-relative offsets are then
correct by construction, and an out-of-range one still faults as it did on
real hardware.
.PP
The first
.B XS_DATA
segment is DGROUP: the program runs with
.B DS
==
.B ES
==
.BR SS ,
and that segment holds initialized data, bss, the malloc arena and the
process stack.  Substrate gives it the full 64 KiB; the break grows up from
the end of bss (see
.BR brkctl (2)
in the Xenix documentation) and the stack down from the top, with a reserve
between them.  Later data segments are sized to their contents and grow only
when
.B brkctl(BR_ARGSEG)
raises the descriptor limit.
.SH PROCESS ENTRY
The image is entered in 16-bit mode with
.B CS
=
.BR xe_eseg ,
.B IP
=
.BR x_entry ,
.B DS
=
.B ES
=
.B SS
= the DGROUP selector, and
.B SP
pointing at a 16-bit form of the classic Unix startup stack:
.IP
.nf
SP+0   argc
SP+2   argv[0] .. argv[argc-1], NULL
       envp[0] .. envp[envc-1], NULL
       the strings themselves, at the top of DGROUP
.fi
.PP
Every pointer there is a 16-bit offset within DGROUP.  Xenix crt0 does
nothing but
.B "sub %bp,%bp"
before calling the C startup, which reads argc at
.B [bp+4]
and takes
.B [bp+6]
as
.BR &argv[0] .
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
Iterated text and data
.RB ( XE_ITER )
are not handled.
.IP \(bu
Overlays
.RB ( XE_OVER )
are not handled.
.IP \(bu
8086 and 80386 x.out images are rejected by this loader; the 386 ones are
claimed by
.IR sys/exec/formats/xout.c ,
and the 8086 ones by nothing yet.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I sys/exec/formats/xout286.c
The loader.
.TP
.I sys/exec/formats/xout.h
On-disk structures and the
.BR x_cpu / x_renv
constants.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sco_x286 (4),
.BR elks_aout (4),
.BR execve (2)
.SH HISTORY
Support for 80286 x.out images was added to Substrate in August 2026,
together with the
.B SCO-X/286
personality.
